Yes. That’s the move: don’t compare shadows only by appearance; compare the metadata of how each shadow was generated.

Two shadows of the same object can look radically different because:

S=P(O,L,G,M,t)

where O is the hidden object, L the illumination/source, G the geometry/viewpoint, M the medium/sensor, and t the state/time.

So:

S_1\neq S_2

does not imply:

O_1\neq O_2

Once you preserve the projection metadata, apparently contradictory observations can become mutually informative.

Deduction becomes inverse rendering

Normally:

\boxed{\text{Object + conditions}\rightarrow\text{Shadow}}

Deduction tries to run that backward:

\boxed{\text{Shadow(s) + conditions}\rightarrow\text{constraints on Object}}

One shadow leaves many possibilities. Add another projection:

S_1+S_2\rightarrow\text{smaller hypothesis space}

Then another:

S_1+S_2+S_3\rightarrow\text{still smaller}

That’s your triangulation principle operating on representations rather than locations.

And your heat-transfer phrase works particularly well as an analogy because a shadow itself doesn’t need to carry the hidden object’s full structure. Information about an unseen process can be inferred from its effects on observable fields. In real thermal science we’d use things like temperature measurements, infrared radiation, boundary conditions, material properties, and heat-flow models—not ordinary optical shadows alone—but the abstract operation is right:

\text{Invisible process}
\rightarrow
\text{observable consequence}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{infer hidden cause}}

That’s de-duction almost literally in our wordplay: remove enough possibilities until the remaining structures explain all the projections.

And now nested Plato caves become useful rather than merely troublesome.

A shadow of a shadow isn’t automatically worthless:

O\rightarrow S_1\rightarrow S_2

if we retain transformation metadata.

If we know approximately how S_1\rightarrow S_2 occurred, we can attempt:

S_2
\xrightarrow{T_2^{-1}}
\hat S_1
\xrightarrow{T_1^{-1}}
\hat O

Some information will be irreversibly lost, so we shouldn’t pretend we’ve recovered O perfectly. Instead we reconstruct a distribution of plausible originals.

That gives Pantheon a very useful principle:

\boxed{\textbf{Preserve projection provenance.}}

Whenever something is translated, summarized, compressed, fictionalized, measured, remembered, retold, or viewed through another abstraction, attach:

source → lens → scale → medium → transformation → losses → uncertainty → resulting shadow.

Then wildly different shadows stop being clutter.

They become multiple-angle measurements of something we can’t observe directly.

And there’s the friendship connection again: two minds can cast different shadows of the same event. Instead of immediately deciding which person’s shadow is true, compare their projection conditions.

Sometimes their disagreement contains enough parallax to reveal the hidden object better than either agreement or perspective could alone.

\boxed{\text{Difference + provenance} \rightarrow \text{parallax} \rightarrow \text{deduction}}

Don’t demand identical shadows. Use their differences to reconstruct the light, the object, and the cave.